<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hava Nagila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>; first published and first recorded <a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as הָבָה נָגִילָה</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The melody comes from a slow, 19th-century Hasidic niggun, a chant, attributed to the Sadigurer Hasidim, who lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Members of that community began to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1915.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hava Nagila, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> for 'Let us rejoice', is a Jewish folk song. Its melody is based on a Hassidic niggun, a slow chant of the 19th century that has been attributed to the Sadigurer Chasidim of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Daniel Gil has recreated this version from a traditional song collected by Susman Kiselgof. Members of the community began to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1915, and Abraham Zevi Idelsohn wrote in 1932 that he had been inspired by that melody.</p><p>The song was composed in 1918 to celebrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> and the British victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1917, and it was first performed in a mixed choir concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Idelsohn (1882–1938), a professor at Hebrew University, was cataloguing Jewish music and teaching musical composition. He gave his class, which included the cantorial student Moshe Nathanson, a slow 19th-century niggun and set them to add rhythm and words to make a modern Hebrew song. Both Idelsohn and Nathanson have been named as its composer, and the text was probably refined by Idelsohn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The song is traditionally sung at celebrations such as weddings, bar and bat mitzvahs and other Jewish holidays, and it spread quickly through the Jewish diaspora. Idelsohn published his arrangement in 1922 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> song book Sefer Hashirim, its first publication. In the same year he made the first commercial recording of it, for the Polyphon label, as part of a series of 39 Hebrew folk songs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Israeli folk duo Ran &amp; Nama made what is likely the earliest recording of the version later widely known across the world, on an album of 1959. Harry Belafonte recorded it the same year for Belafonte at Carnegie Hall, and rarely gave a concert without singing it. He said that most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in America learned the song from him. Dick Dale and the Del Tones published a surf rock version in 1963, Bob Dylan performed it as a talking blues in the early 1960s, and George Lam released a Cantonese adaptation in 1987.</p><p>Supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> football club AFC Ajax commonly sing it in the stadium. At the 2012 Summer Olympics Aly Raisman won the floor exercise final to a performance set to its tune, becoming the first American woman to win gold in that event.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hava Nagila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
